      "problem": "${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
